    "evidence_text": "METHODS AND FINDINGS We prospectively investigated combination chemotherapy followed by antiretroviral therapy for this disorder.\n\nThe epidemiology of sexually transmitted co-infections in HIV-positive and HIV-negative African-Caribbean women in Toronto\n\nMETHODS We recruited women based on HIV status through a Toronto community health centre.\n\nDifferences in prevalence were assessed for statistical significance using chi-square.   \n\n\nCONCLUSIONS Classical STIs were infrequent in this clinic-based population of African-Caribbean women in Toronto.\n\nNatural history of adult T-cell leukemia/lymphoma and approaches to therapy\n\nAfter cell-to-cell transmission, HTLV-I increases its viral genome by de novo infection and proliferation of infected cells.\n\nProliferation of infected cells is clonal and persistent in vivo.\n\nSuch loss of Tax expression enables ATL cells to escape the host immune system.\n\nDespite advances in support and the development of novel treatment agents, the prognosis for ATLL remains poor.\n\nSeven of 8 women who reported Kaposi's sarcoma had HHV-8 antibodies.\n\nIn this cohort of North American women, HHV-8 infection was associated with HIV infection, drug use, and risky sexual behavior.\n\nClinical and laboratory features of adult T-cell leukaemia lymphoma in Barbados.\n\nTwo patients had a past history of tropical spastic paraparesis/HTLV I associated myelopathy (TSP/HAM).\n\nHTLV-I/II associated disease in England and Wales, 1993-7: retrospective review of serology requests.\n\nHTLV-I infection is endemic in Japan, the Caribbean, Africa, and Melanesia and is found among immigrants from these regions in Europe.\n\n\u2026\n\nAdult T-cell leukemia/lymphoma in London: clinical experience of 21 cases.\n\nAdult T-cell leukemia/lymphoma (ATLL) is uncommon in the United Kingdom and has so far been restricted to people of Afro-Caribbean extraction.\n\nClinical presentations were similar to those of the disease in HTLV-I-endemic areas.\n\nHowever, median survival was only 5.5 months.\n\nDisease progression and opportunistic infection were the major causes of treatment failure and death.\n\nThree patients (14%) relapsed in the central nervous system (CNS).\n\nOur cases confirm the profound immunosuppression in ATLL.\n\nIsolation and partial characterization of an unusual human immunodeficiency retrovirus from two persons of west-central African origin.\n\nFurthermore, nucleic acid hybridization experiments were capable of discriminating between the virus types.\n\nHowever, it is not known if this apoptosis occurs in infected or uninfected T cells.\n\nWe treated 15 ATLL patients with IFN and AZT.\n\nEleven patients had acute ATLL, two had lymphoma and two smouldering ATLL, with progression.",
